cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Three improved lower bounds for hypergraph Ramsey numbers: R(4,4,4;3) >= 84, R(4,6;3) >= 64, R(5,5;4) >= 36"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
version: "1.0"
date-released: "2026-09-10"
repository-code: "https://github.com/SeverinVisionary/hypergraph-ramsey-bounds"
authors:
  - given-names: Hanyu
    family-names: Yang
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-0419-4070"
abstract: >-
  Explicit group-invariant colourings improving three entries of section 7.1(a)
  of Radziszowski's dynamic survey Small Ramsey Numbers (DS1, revision 18).
  A 3-colouring of the triples of an 83-set with no monochromatic K_4 gives
  R(4,4,4;3) >= 84, against the recorded 79; a 2-colouring of the triples of a
  63-set gives R(4,6;3) >= 64, against 63; and a 2-colouring of the 4-subsets of
  a 35-set gives R(5,5;4) >= 36, against 35. Each colouring is invariant under an
  affine or holomorph group and is presented as a table over group orbits -- 27,
  120 and 107 numbers respectively -- from which the full colouring is
  regenerated without a solver. Each is verified by complete enumeration using a
  checker with a different implementation from the search. Five UNSAT symmetry classes of the
  n = 63 tower can be re-certified by rerunning the solver and replaying its
  proof with a separate RUP/RAT checker; the proof clauses themselves are not
  deposited. An accompanying
  obstruction theorem shows that an affine group whose order equals C(p,2) and
  whose multiplier subgroup has index two and omits -1 (equivalently, within
  that index-two family, p = 3 mod 4) acts regularly
  on unordered pairs, so adjoining a fixed point forces every triple through
  that point into one orbit and hence one colour. For the diagonal problem
  R(4,...,4;3) in m colours that leaves the base needing an (m-1)-colouring of
  its triples with no monochromatic K_4^(3), which is impossible only once the
  base reaches or exceeds the (m-1)-colour Ramsey number: at m = 3 that threshold is
  R(4,4;3) = 13 and both towers clear it, while at m = 4 it is R(4,4,4;3)
  itself and p = 79 does not -- adjoining a fourth colour to the deposited
  79-point witness gives a good invariant colouring of the extension, so the
  conclusion genuinely needs the threshold and is not implied by the two group
  conditions alone. The pair-regular class of each tower here is the
  index-two multiplier subgroup -- C_41 at p = 83, C_39 at p = 79, in neither
  case the top class of the tower -- and each of those base classes holds a
  witness; what the theorem explains without computation is that adjoining a
  fixed point empties them, so Z_83 : C_41 + 1 fixed and Z_79 : C_39 + 1 fixed
  are UNSAT. The order condition alone is insufficient. When p is congruent to
  1 modulo 4, the index-two multiplier subgroup contains -1 and there are two
  pair-orbits. The three-colour fixed-point classes at p = 13 and p = 37 are
  satisfiable. These are specific counterexamples to an order-only obstruction,
  not a classification of all such primes or a general independence result for
  every hypothesis. Novelty is provisional: both incumbent bounds are unpublished personal
  communications, which were not obtained through the bibliographic sources
  searched; that is a search boundary rather than a claim that they can never
  be obtained.
keywords:
  - Ramsey theory
  - hypergraph Ramsey numbers
  - 3-uniform hypergraphs
  - lower bounds
  - SAT solving
  - symmetry reduction
  - group orbits
  - certified computation
  - DRAT proofs
